er.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-24T00:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Uncompahgre Plateau/Dallas Divide; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; Arches/Grand Flat; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.e0dbcacb680ad71bb79dff518cb60ff22ebdb688.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.e0dbcacb680ad71bb79dff518cb60ff22ebdb688.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued October 22 at 9:44PM MDT until October 23 at 2:00AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues.\n\n* WHERE...Western Archuleta County in southwestern Colorado...\nSouthern Hinsdale County in southwestern Colorado...\nSouthern La Plata County in southwestern Colorado...\n\n* WHEN...Until 200 AM MDT.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying\nand flood-prone locations is imminent or occurring. Streams\ncontinue to rise due to excess runoff from earlier rainfall.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 939 PM MDT, Doppler radar and automated rain gauges indicated\nheavy rain. Flooding is ongoing or expected to begin shortly in\nthe warned area. Between 1 and 2 inches of rain have fallen.\n- This includes the following streams and drainages...\nLa Plata River, Animas River, Vallecito Creek, Taylor Creek, Los\nPinos River, Florida River, and First Fork Piedra River.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nDurango, La Plata, Falfa, Hesperus, Bondad, Breen, Mayday, Kline,\nMarvel, Redmesa and Vallecito.\n- Flooding along the Animas River, including areas on the north\nside of Durango, is ongoing or expected to begin shortly. Rivers\nand streams will continue to rise through the night, with peak\nflow expected Friday afternoon.\n- http://www.weather.g